"── 116_winui_pixels.mst — WG8 / SM0: the pixel plane ────────────────────
 docs/sprints/upstream_review_2026-08-12.md. Upstream's thesis, ported: *the
 VM writes video memory, it does not send messages*.

 WHAT CHANGED, and it is a whole class of cost rather than a speed-up. The
 shape this replaces — build a ByteArray, hand it to a `blit:` primitive, copy
 it out into a fresh Vec, push that through a mutex to another thread, memcpy
 it into a slot, upload — is three full-frame copies and an allocation PER
 FRAME. Here the guest stores bytes straight into the buffer the renderer will
 draw, and the only copy left is the upload the GPU needs anyway.

 THE HONEST DIFFERENCE from the Mac: Metal's shared-storage buffers give a
 PERSISTENT pointer whose bytes the GPU samples, so upstream has zero copies.
 D3D11 hands out a mapped pointer only between Map and Unmap, which cannot be
 given to the guest to hold across frames — so this is one copy where the Mac
 has none. `winui_render`'s own comment carries the same note; neither side
 pretends otherwise.

 THE SHAPE COMES FROM THE RENDERER, ALWAYS. Width, height and STRIDE are
 asked for, never assumed — and that is the standing caution of the WG8 review
 made concrete. A pixel plane is the one place in this port where the GUEST
 computes an address, which is exactly the two-authorities arrangement that
 cost WG6c four defects. The cell grid escaped it by making the renderer the
 only side that knows where a pixel lives; a pixel plane cannot, so the
 discipline moves to the buffer's shape.

 BGRA, because that is the swapchain's format (`DXGI_FORMAT_B8G8R8A8_UNORM`)
 and a plane in any other order would be converted on every frame — which is
 the copy this file exists to avoid."

Object subclass: WinPixels [
    <classVars: Available>

    WinPixels class >> rawPlane: hwnd <Integer> w: w <Integer> h: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderPixelPlane library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g g g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderPixelPlane'
    ]
    WinPixels class >> rawStride: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderPixelStride library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderPixelStride'
    ]
    WinPixels class >> rawIsUma: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderIsUma library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderIsUma'
    ]
    WinPixels class >> rawDrop: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderDropPixelPlane library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderDropPixelPlane'
    ]

    WinPixels class >> available ^ <Boolean> [
        Available isNil ifTrue: [
            Available := [ self rawStride: 0. true ] on: Error do: [ :e | false ] ].
        ^Available
    ]

    WinPixels class >> planeFor: hwnd <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "The buffer's ADDRESS. It CHANGES when the size does, so this is asked
         for per frame rather than cached — one call, and it removes a whole
         class of dangling-pointer bug that would present as garbage on screen
         or as a fault inside the renderer."
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self rawPlane: hwnd w: w h: h ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> strideFor: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self rawStride: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> unifiedMemory: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "1 on a unified-memory device — Snapdragon X says 1 — 0 on a discrete
         adapter, -1 with no renderer. The fact comes from CheckFeatureSupport
         on the DEVICE, so it is true of the machine actually drawing, not of
         the machine the code was written on."
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 - 1 ].
        ^[ self rawIsUma: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 - 1 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> dropFor: hwnd <Integer> [
        self available ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        [ self rawDrop: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── SM4: the palette, as memory ──────────────────────────────────────
     An INDEXED plane stores one byte per pixel naming a slot in a 256-entry
     table, and the renderer expands the two on its own side of the seam.

     WHY THIS IS A DESIGN and not a compression trick: the cost of re-colouring
     the screen stops depending on the screen. A cycling effect writes 256 words
     a frame instead of `w * h` — 256 against 19,200 at 160x120 — which is the
     difference between something a Smalltalk loop can drive at frame rate and
     something it cannot. Upstream's copper bars are the same claim with a
     per-scanline palette; this is the flat version, and the one WG8 needs.

     TWO CALLS, not a flag: `planeFor:` makes a plane, `indexPlaneFor:` turns it
     indexed. So the direct-BGRA path is untouched by SM4 existing, and choosing
     indices is visible at the call site rather than hidden in an argument."

    WinPixels class >> rawIndexPlane: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderIndexPlane library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderIndexPlane'
    ]
    WinPixels class >> rawIndexStride: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderIndexStride library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderIndexStride'
    ]
    WinPixels class >> rawPalette: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderPalette library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderPalette'
    ]
    WinPixels class >> rawPaletteLen: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        <primitive: FFI function: #MacvmRenderPaletteLen library: #'winui_render.dll' ret: #g args: #(g)>
        ^self primitiveFailed: 'MacvmRenderPaletteLen'
    ]

    WinPixels class >> indexPlaneFor: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "The index buffer's address, or 0 if this pane has no plane yet."
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self rawIndexPlane: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> indexStrideFor: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self rawIndexStride: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> paletteFor: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self rawPalette: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> paletteLenFor: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "ASKED FOR, like the stride. There is no index a guest can write that
         reads off the end of the table, and this is how it stays that way when
         the table's size one day is not 256."
        (self available not or: [ hwnd = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^[ self rawPaletteLen: hwnd ] on: Error do: [ :e | 0 ]
    ]

    WinPixels class >> indexAt: base <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                       stride: stride <Integer> put: slot <Integer> [
        "ONE BYTE, not a word — an index plane is `w` bytes per row, so a `u32`
         store here would write four pixels and look like a stripe."
        WinArena u8At: base + (y * stride) + x put: (slot bitAnd: 255).
        ^self
    ]

    WinPixels class >> palette: base <Integer> at: slot <Integer> put: bgra <Integer> [
        WinArena u32At: base + (slot * 4) put: bgra.
        ^self
    ]

    "── one pixel ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Written through `WinArena u32At:put:` — a store into foreign memory, the
     same mechanism `Alien forAddress:size:` gives and the same one the cell
     grid uses. There is no primitive here and no message: THIS is the whole
     of `the VM writes video memory`."

    WinPixels class >> at: base <Integer> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer> stride: stride <Integer>
                       put: bgra <Integer> [
        WinArena u32At: base + (y * stride) + (x * 4) put: bgra.
        ^self
    ]

    WinPixels class >> transparentBackground ^ <Integer> [
        "The background that means DO NOT FILL — `BG_TRANSPARENT` in
         `winui_render`, above the COLORREF range so it cannot collide with a
         colour anyone means. Clearing a grid to this is what lets a HUD sit
         over live pixels: the glyphs still draw, the cells behind them do not.

         Without it the two planes cannot compose at all — `draw_grid` fills
         every cell's background, so a plane under a full-width grid shows only
         in the margin the grid does not reach. Measured exactly that way: the
         first plasma frame was bands of colour down the right edge and along
         the bottom, and white everywhere the grid was."
        ^16r01000000
    ]

    WinPixels class >> bgraR: r <Integer> g: g <Integer> b: b <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "BGRA as one word, alpha opaque. The swapchain is B8G8R8A8, so blue is
         the LOW byte — the same packing `colorRefPacksBlueHigh` pins for
         COLORREF, one byte order along."
        ^((b bitAnd: 255) bitShift: 0)
            + ((g bitAnd: 255) bitShift: 8)
            + ((r bitAnd: 255) bitShift: 16)
            + 16rFF000000
    ]
]

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: PlasmaPhase CanvasFrames CanvasMode IndexFieldDrawn CanvasAnimating LastFrameMs>

    "-- the CANVAS view, which is where pixels belong --------------------
     The first version of this demo drew plasma into the EDITOR's pane, and the
     author's response was the right one: that is a strange place to test
     pixels. `docs/SPRINTS.md`'s WG8 row always said Canvas, and a view whose
     whole job is a pixel plane is both the honest home for it and the better
     proof — the Editor pane happening to also work would say nothing about
     whether a Canvas does.

     It is the fourth consumer of WG6d-3's per-window renderers, and the first
     that is mostly PIXELS rather than mostly cells: the plane underneath, a
     cell HUD over it, neither knowing about the other."

    WinShell class >> canvasViewName ^ <Symbol> [ ^#canvas ]
    WinShell class >> canvasPaneName ^ <Symbol> [ ^#canvasPane ]

    WinShell class >> canvasPaneHwnd ^ <Integer> [
        | c |
        c := self controlNamed: self canvasPaneName.
        ^c isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ c handle ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> buildCanvasPane [
        (self controlNamed: self canvasPaneName) ifNotNil: [ :x | ^self ].
        self
            addControl: self canvasPaneName
            class: self className
            text: ''
            style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER'))
                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_CLIPSIBLINGS'))
            exStyle: 0.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> showCanvasPane: aBool <Boolean> [
        (self controlNamed: self canvasPaneName) ifNotNil: [ :c | c show: aBool ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> layoutCanvasPane: content <Array> [
        (self controlNamed: self canvasPaneName) ifNotNil: [ :c | c moveTo: content ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> canvasSize ^ <Array> [
        "The pane's client size in PIXELS. A canvas is one pixel per pixel, so
         there is no cell arithmetic in this method at all."
        | c r |
        c := self controlNamed: self canvasPaneName.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 1 with: 1 ].
        r := [ c screenRect ] on: Error do: [ :e | nil ].
        r isNil ifTrue: [ ^Array with: 1 with: 1 ].
        ^Array
            with: (((r at: 3) - (r at: 1)) max: 1)
            with: (((r at: 4) - (r at: 2)) max: 1)
    ]

    WinShell class >> canvasPlaneWidth ^ <Integer> [
        "PER MODE, because the plane's size is a budget and the modes spend
         differently. Plasma and the palette write cheap pixels — 320x240.
         Julia ITERATES per pixel; at 320x240 a frame cost ~100ms of UI-thread
         time, which is longer than the 33ms frame budget — the thread never
         idled and the control port starved. At 160x120 it fits, and the GPU's
         point-sampler stretches it to the pane; big pixels are what a
         computed plane honestly looks like.

         The durable fix is the next sprint: the demo AS a shader on the
         Adreno (upstream's GamePane>>shader:), where per-pixel work belongs."
        ^self canvasMode == #julia ifTrue: [ 160 ] ifFalse: [ 320 ] ]
    WinShell class >> canvasPlaneHeight ^ <Integer> [
        ^self canvasMode == #julia ifTrue: [ 120 ] ifFalse: [ 240 ] ]


    WinShell class >> canvasFrames ^ <Integer> [
        ^CanvasFrames isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ CanvasFrames ] ]

    "── the animation loop ──────────────────────────────────────────────
     Until WG10 there was none. The Canvas rendered on `WM_PAINT` and when
     something evaluated `renderCanvas`, which means a demo whose whole point is
     that it MOVES sat still — and a still plasma is indistinguishable from a
     broken one, which is the failure `plasmaFrame` was written to avoid and
     then reintroduced by having nothing drive it.

     ONE FRAME PER DRAIN PASS, and then ASK FOR ANOTHER PASS. The heartbeat is
     50 ms, so animating on the heartbeat alone caps at 20 fps regardless of how
     fast a frame is; `ReaskOnce` makes the pump come straight back once the
     message queue is empty, so the rate is the frame time and input still wins.
     A `while true` here would be the same frames and a dead window."

    WinShell class >> canvasAnimating ^ <Boolean> [
        ^CanvasAnimating == true ]

    WinShell class >> canvasTimerId ^ <Integer> [ ^55458 ]

    WinShell class >> canvasFpsTarget ^ <Integer> [
        "30, and it is a REQUEST RATE, not a speed limit the loop tries to
         beat. The author's words when the first loop ran free at 200 fps and
         saturated the UI thread: we are meant to request the frame at 30 or
         60 fps. A demo that renders faster than anyone can see it is spending
         the interactive thread on frames the panel will drop."
        ^30 ]

    WinShell class >> canvasAnimating: aBool <Boolean> ^ <Boolean> [
        "A DEDICATED TIMER, not a spin. `askForAnotherPass` + a posted wake
         re-enters the drain the moment the queue is empty — measured saturating
         the UI thread so thoroughly the control port timed out. The timer fires
         WM_TIMER at ~16ms; the drain runs only when the request flag is armed;
         and `serviceCanvasAnimation` renders only when a frame is DUE. Between
         frames the thread is genuinely idle, which is what the busy indicator
         was reporting the absence of."
        CanvasAnimating := aBool.
        self isOpen ifTrue: [
            aBool
                ifTrue: [
                    WinApi setTimer: self hwndValue id: self canvasTimerId
                        elapse: 16 proc: 0.
                    self askForAnotherPass ]
                ifFalse: [
                    WinApi killTimer: self hwndValue id: self canvasTimerId ] ].
        ^self canvasAnimating
    ]

    WinShell class >> serviceCanvasAnimation [
        "One frame IF ONE IS DUE, then re-arm and go idle. Cheapest possible
         no on every pass where nothing is animating — three tests and out.

         THE SHAPE: the canvas timer ticks at ~16ms; every tick runs a drain
         pass because the request flag below stays armed; and the clock check
         here turns ticks into frames at `canvasFpsTarget`. No posted wake —
         a posted wake re-enters the drain the instant the queue empties,
         which is a spin wearing a schedule, and it saturated the UI thread
         until the control port timed out. Between frames the thread idles in
         GetMessage, which is where an interactive thread belongs."
        | now due |
        self canvasAnimating ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        self activeView == self canvasViewName ifFalse: [ ^self ].
        self canvasPaneHwnd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        now := Time millisecondClockValue.
        due := LastFrameMs isNil
            or: [ now - LastFrameMs >= (1000 // self canvasFpsTarget) ].
        due ifTrue: [
            LastFrameMs := now.
            self renderCanvas ].
        self askForAnotherPass.
        ^self
    ]

    "-- the demo ---------------------------------------------------------
     Plasma: cheap, obviously wrong if the arithmetic is wrong, and it MOVES —
     so a frozen frame reads as frozen rather than as a still image. Upstream's
     45d does the same job for the same reason."

    WinShell class >> plasmaPhase ^ <Integer> [
        ^PlasmaPhase isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ PlasmaPhase ] ]

    WinShell class >> drawPlasmaOn: hwnd <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "One frame, stored STRAIGHT into the renderer's own buffer. Pure
         arithmetic over (x, y, phase) — no allocation, and nothing retained
         between frames but one integer."
        | base stride ph |
        base := WinPixels planeFor: hwnd width: w height: h.
        base = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        stride := WinPixels strideFor: hwnd.
        stride = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ph := self plasmaPhase.
        "ROW BY ROW, and the row is a METHOD — but NOT for the reason first
         written here. The original claim (`tier-up counts method entries, not
         back-edges; there is no OSR`) was wrong: this VM does OSR, counts
         back-edges, and would have rescued this loop in place. The real rule,
         measured and corrected in `119_winui_demos.mst`s note: a hot loop
         tiers only in an INSTALLED method of argc <= 5. This one is both, so
         it now has two roads to compiled code — the invocation counter after
         the first frame, and OSR at back-edge 10,001 within it."
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :y |
            self plasmaRow: y base: base stride: stride width: w phase: ph ].
        PlasmaPhase := ph + 4.
        ^true
    ]

    "── SM4's demo: the palette, cycling ────────────────────────────────
     The index field is written ONCE. Every frame after that changes nothing but
     256 palette words, and the whole screen moves. That is the claim SM4 makes,
     and running it beside the plasma — which stores every pixel, every frame —
     is what makes the difference visible rather than asserted."

    WinShell class >> canvasMode ^ <Symbol> [
        ^CanvasMode isNil ifTrue: [ #plasma ] ifFalse: [ CanvasMode ] ]

    WinShell class >> indexFieldDrawn ^ <Boolean> [
        "Read by `gate-wg8`. That the field is written ONCE is the whole of
         SM4's claim, and it is the one part a picture cannot show — a demo
         that rewrote every index every frame would look identical."
        ^IndexFieldDrawn == true ]

    WinShell class >> canvasMode: aSymbol <Symbol> ^ <Symbol> [
        "Switching modes DROPS the plane, because the two modes disagree about
         what the buffer contains — an indexed plane reinterpreted as BGRA is a
         quarter-width smear, which looks like a stride bug and is not one."
        aSymbol = self canvasMode ifFalse: [
            WinPixels dropFor: self canvasPaneHwnd.
            IndexFieldDrawn := false.
            CanvasMode := aSymbol ].
        ^self canvasMode
    ]

    WinShell class >> paintIndexFieldOn: hwnd <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "The field, ONCE. Slots rather than colours — nothing here decides what
         anything looks like, which is exactly why the palette can."
        | ix stride |
        IndexFieldDrawn == true ifTrue: [ ^true ].
        ix := WinPixels indexPlaneFor: hwnd.
        ix = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        stride := WinPixels indexStrideFor: hwnd.
        stride = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        0 to: h - 1 do: [ :y |
            0 to: w - 1 do: [ :x |
                "Concentric-ish rings from the middle, in integer arithmetic —
                 `dx*dx + dy*dy` shifted down until it lands inside a byte."
                | dx dy |
                dx := x - (w // 2).
                dy := (y - (h // 2)) * 2.
                WinPixels indexAt: ix x: x y: y stride: stride
                    put: (((dx * dx) + (dy * dy)) // 24) ] ].
        IndexFieldDrawn := true.
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> cyclePaletteOn: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "256 stores. THE ENTIRE COST OF A FRAME — the field is untouched and
         every pixel on screen changes colour anyway."
        | pal n ph |
        pal := WinPixels paletteFor: hwnd.
        pal = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        n := WinPixels paletteLenFor: hwnd.
        n = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        ph := self plasmaPhase.
        0 to: n - 1 do: [ :i |
            | v |
            v := (i * 8 + ph) bitAnd: 255.
            WinPixels palette: pal at: i put:
                (WinPixels bgraR: v g: ((v * 2) bitAnd: 255) b: 255 - v) ].
        PlasmaPhase := ph + 3.
        ^true
    ]

    WinShell class >> drawCanvasContentOn: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "ONE PLACE THE MODE IS DECIDED. `renderCanvas` owns the pane, the grid
         and the HUD; what goes in the plane is chosen here and nowhere else,
         which is what lets WG10 add demos without touching the render path.

         Anything this file does not know is handed to `drawGalleryOn:` — a
         no-op until `119_winui_demos.mst` defines it, so the Canvas works with
         or without the gallery loaded."
        self canvasMode == #palette ifTrue: [
            "The plane must exist before it can be indexed — SM4 is a second
             call on an SM0 plane, never a replacement for one."
            (WinPixels planeFor: hwnd width: self canvasPlaneWidth height: self canvasPlaneHeight) = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
            (self paintIndexFieldOn: hwnd width: self canvasPlaneWidth height: self canvasPlaneHeight) ifFalse: [ ^false ].
            ^self cyclePaletteOn: hwnd ].
        self canvasMode == #plasma ifTrue: [
            ^self drawPlasmaOn: hwnd width: self canvasPlaneWidth height: self canvasPlaneHeight ].
        ^self drawGalleryOn: hwnd
    ]

    WinShell class >> drawGalleryOn: hwnd <Integer> ^ <Boolean> [
        "WG10 overrides. Answering false here means an unknown mode draws
         nothing rather than drawing the last mode's buffer, which would be a
         stale frame presented as a current one."
        ^false
    ]

    WinShell class >> canvasLabel ^ <String> [
        "The HUD names the COST, because that is what the modes differ in and it
         is not visible from the picture."
        self canvasMode == #palette ifTrue: [
            ^'CANVAS palette - 256 stores/frame, 19200 pixels move' ].
        ^'CANVAS plasma - 19200 stores/frame, direct BGRA'
    ]

    WinShell class >> plasmaRow: y <Integer> base: base <Integer> stride: stride <Integer>
                      width: w <Integer> phase: ph <Integer> [
        "Integer-only: a per-pixel Float would allocate on a tagged-integer VM,
         and this runs w times a row, h rows a frame."
        | v b2 |
        b2 := (y * 2) + ph.
        0 to: w - 1 do: [ :x |
            v := ((x * 3) + b2) bitAnd: 255.
            WinPixels at: base x: x y: y stride: stride
                put: (WinPixels bgraR: v g: 255 - v b: ((x + y + ph) bitAnd: 255)) ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> renderCanvas ^ <Boolean> [
        "BOTH PLANES, which is the claim WG8 actually makes: pixels underneath,
         a text HUD over them, neither knowing about the other.

         The plane is a FIXED 160x120 stretched to the pane by the renderer,
         nearest-neighbour — cheap at any window size, and the pixels look like
         pixels rather than being interpolated into values the guest never
         wrote. The grid is cleared to `transparentBackground`, so every cell
         the HUD does not fill lets the plane through while the cells it does
         fill still draw their glyphs."
        | hwnd cw ch cols rows base label col |
        hwnd := self canvasPaneHwnd.
        hwnd = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        (WinRender cellWidthFor: hwnd) = 0 ifTrue: [
            (WinRender attach: hwnd points: 9 dpi: self editorMetricsDpi)
                ifFalse: [ ^false ] ].
        (self drawCanvasContentOn: hwnd) ifFalse: [ ^false ].
        cw := WinRender cellWidthFor: hwnd.
        ch := WinRender cellHeightFor: hwnd.
        (cw = 0 or: [ ch = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        cols := (((self canvasSize at: 1) // cw) max: 1).
        rows := (((self canvasSize at: 2) // ch) max: 1).
        base := WinRender gridFor: hwnd cols: cols rows: rows.
        base = 0 ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        WinRender clear: hwnd to: 16rFFFFFF on: WinPixels transparentBackground.
        "`WinShell canvasMode: #plasma | #palette | #life | #julia` in the
         Workspace switches; each mode owns its own line via `canvasLabel`."
        label := self canvasLabel , ' - frame ' , (self canvasFrames + 1) printString.
        "The label's OWN cells are opaque; every other cell in the grid is not.
         White-on-transparent was legible over the plasma and unreadable over a
         cycling palette — there is no ink colour that contrasts with a field
         whose colours are the thing moving. So the HUD gets a strip, which is
         what a HUD normally is, and the composition claim is unchanged: one row
         of the grid is opaque and every other row lets the plane through."
        col := 0.
        label do: [ :chr |
            col < cols ifTrue: [
                WinRender at: base col: col row: 0 cols: cols
                    put: chr asInteger
                    fg: 16rFFFFFF
                    bg: 16r303030 ].
            col := col + 1 ].
        WinRender caretFor: hwnd col: 0 row: 0 visible: false.
        CanvasFrames := self canvasFrames + 1.
        ^WinRender present: hwnd
    ]
]
